affordable resource,

00:02:54.630 --> 00:02:56.520
and Pi Pi controls it all.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.250
It's a perfect example of
how the market can find ways

00:02:59.250 --> 00:03:01.470
to profit off of just about anything.

00:03:01.470 --> 00:03:04.110
In this instance, Pi Pi
is using a real problem

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:06.600
affecting the life and health
of a community, a drought,

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:09.600
to create a great business
opportunity for himself.

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:11.040
And the show is clearly pointing out

00:03:11.040 --> 00:03:13.620
that this happens all the
time in the real world.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:16.410
Just ask anyone who bought
stock in Moderna and Pfizer

00:03:16.410 --> 00:03:17.550
in the spring of 2020,

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:19.110
which I don't know if that
makes you a bad person,

00:03:19.110 --> 00:03:21.300
but it felt weird, it felt
weird when people did that.

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:22.867
According to scholar, David Harvey,

00:03:22.867 --> 00:03:25.470
"Crises shake our mental
conceptions of the world

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:27.270
and our place in it to the very core.

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:30.270
And we, as restless
participants and inhabitants

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.340
of this new emerging world, have to adapt,

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:35.280
through coercion of consent,
to the new state of things."

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.410
In other words, crises
are a great opportunity

00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:40.560
for those with power to rework
things for their benefit.

00:03:40.560 --> 00:03:42.900
And we see this in Matt
Damon's favorite hobby,

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.590
cryptocurrency trading, which
South Park continues to roast.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:48.300
Well, it might not be as exploitative

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.190
as holding water supplies hostage,

00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:51.930
lots of folks without money to spare

00:03:51.930 --> 00:03:54.570
have lost ridiculous
amounts buying crypto,

00:03:54.570 --> 00:03:56.340
and celebrity-ridden ads for crypto

00:03:56.340 --> 00:03:59.280
are clearly playing in to
current economic anxieties.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:00.810
And while crypto is a good example,

00:04:00.810 --> 00:04:03.120
this is just what the market
economy does in general,

00:04:03.120 --> 00:04:06.450
namely, finds a way to
profit off human necessities,

00:04:06.450 --> 00:04:07.890
food, housing, medicine,

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:10.560
while creating desire
for things we don't need,

00:04:10.560 --> 00:04:13.170
multiple streaming services,
speculative currencies,

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:14.910
fake boobs for our moms.

00:04:14.910 --> 00:04:17.460
And Damon is back for The
Streaming Wars Part 2.

00:04:17.460 --> 00:04:19.350
And this time he and his celebrity friends

00:04:19.350 --> 00:04:20.700
are getting paid so much

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:23.280
that they're willing
to drink recycled piss.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.470
It's shockingly not too
far off from reality.

00:04:25.470 --> 00:04:27.900
Recently, fellow
influencer, Kim Kardashian,

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:30.510
has told interviewers
she'd eat her own (beep)

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:32.190
if it would keep her looking young.

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:35.820
I drink my own piss and
it keeps me looking old.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:37.680
Um. (laughs)

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:38.880
Of course, these celebrities

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:40.620
can endorse things like cryptocurrencies

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.350
because their finances
are already good to go.

00:04:43.350 --> 00:04:44.910
It'll be the naive consumer

00:04:44.910 --> 00:04:47.070
who risked their
financial future on crypto

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:49.260
just because Matt Damon told them that--

00:04:49.260 --> 00:04:50.520
- Fortune favors the brave!

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.920
- Fortune favors the brave. (gags)

00:04:52.920 --> 00:04:55.410
- At least when you buy a
Jennifer Lopez endorsed shampoo,

00:04:55.410 --> 00:04:57.270
it's not gonna ruin your retirement plan.

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:59.850
But instead of focusing on
crypto, this episode dives

00:04:59.850 --> 00:05:02.010
into the depths of Pi Pi's piss palace,

00:05:02.010 --> 00:05:04.710
which exists solely because he
took out the other streamers

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.680
to give himself a stranglehold
over South Park's water,

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:09.630
exacerbating an environmental crisis

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:13.140
to help turn a public good,
water, into profitable business.

00:05:13.140 --> 00:05:14.460
And now he's poised to make bank

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.800
by selling people's piss back to them.

00:05:16.800 --> 00:05:18.960
And this is what writer
and activist, Naomi Klein,

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:20.550
calls "disaster capitalism."

00:05:20.550 --> 00:05:22.830
Orchestrated raids on the public's sphere

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:24.570
in the wake of catastrophic events,

00:05:24.570 --> 00:05:26.670
combined with the treatment of disasters

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.620
as exciting market opportunities.

00:05:28.620 --> 00:05:30.780
And Pi Pi is far from the first person

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:33.180
to spin (beep) situations into major coin.

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:36.300
America has pulled a much
larger version of Pi Pi's move

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.610
in several South and
Central American countries.

00:05:38.610 --> 00:05:41.670
- That is somebody who has
helped planned coup d'état.

00:05:41.670 --> 00:05:43.560
Not here, but you know, other places.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:45.330
- Like Chile, where
they helped the military

00:05:45.330 --> 00:05:48.030
carry out a violent coup in 1973

00:05:48.030 --> 00:05:49.530
on behalf of Augusto Pinochet,

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.320
who would go on to rule
with stunning brutality.

00:05:52.320 --> 00:05:54.630
After the coup, Milton
Friedman and his acolytes

00:05:54.630 --> 00:05:56.130
from the Chicago School of Economics

00:05:56.130 --> 00:05:59.220
helped install a shiny
new free market economy.

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.380
Still reeling from political violence,

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:03.450
Chile had the perfect conditions

00:06:03.450 --> 00:06:06.180
for what Friedman called
economic "shock treatment."

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.430
Fearful of communism and eager to exploit

00:06:08.430 --> 00:06:10.590
economic opportunities
in countries like Chile,

00:06:10.590 --> 00:06:13.260
America repeatedly backed military regimes

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:15.540
that privatized public industries,

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:18.210
turning public wealth into private profit.

00:06:18.210 --> 00:06:21.660
And, rest assured, American
corporations saw plenty of money

00:06:21.660 --> 00:06:23.250
to be made in these new markets.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:27.000
And yet, the actual economic
results were catastrophic.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.750
As client rights, Chile's
economy shrank 15%

00:06:30.750 --> 00:06:32.760
in the first year of "shock therapy,"

00:06:32.760 --> 00:06:36.600
while unemployment grew
from 3% to a staggering 20%.

00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.090
And America used this same playbook

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:40.410
during the War on Terror.

00:06:40.410 --> 00:06:42.540
According to Klein,
the Bush administration

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:44.070
used the trauma of 9/11

00:06:44.070 --> 00:06:46.260
to exploit all the economic opportunities

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:48.780
that come with decades-long wars.

00:06:48.780 --> 00:06:50.940
They essentially launched a new economy.

00:06:50.940 --> 00:06:53.550
- Because the parameters of this economy

00:06:53.550 --> 00:06:55.020
were extraordinarily wide.

00:06:55.020 --> 00:06:56.280
This is an endless war.

00:06:56.280 --> 00:07:00.180
We were told that the enemy
could not be reasoned with.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:01.380
There could be no diplomacy.

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:02.370
There could be no discussion.

00:07:02.370 --> 00:07:04.380
There could only be attack.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:05.970
And that it would not end

00:07:05.970 --> 00:07:08.130
until evil was defeated everywhere, right?

00:07:08.130 --> 00:07:10.200
So if you think of it as a business plan,

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:11.700
it could hardly be more profitable.

00:07:11.700 --> 00:07:12.750
Because basically what you're saying

00:07:12.750 --> 00:07:14.580
is you've got this new market,

00:07:14.580 --> 00:07:19.560
it's never gonna end, and
we have unlimited funds

00:07:19.560 --> 00:07:21.450
to finance this new market.

00:07:21.450 --> 00:07:24.000
- And in the case of Pi
Pi's empire, we've got shock

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:26.430
and a seemingly limitless market.

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:27.960
So what can we do when faced

00:07:27.960 --> 00:07:30.600
with the overwhelming forces
of disaster capitalism?

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.460
Klein generally concludes

00:07:32.460 --> 00:07:35.280
that when unfettered private
markets control society,

00:07:35.280 --> 00:07:37.020
it can empower bad actors.

00:07:37.020 --> 00:07:38.670
And no, she doesn't mean Gwyneth Paltrow.

00:07:38.670 --> 00:07:39.810
And that to counter this,

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:42.000
we need both greater
regulation of industry,

00:07:42.000 --> 00:07:45.240
and an economy where certain
industries and infrastructures

00:07:45.240 --> 00:07:46.320
are publicly owned,

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:47.850
where certain human needs

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:50.010
are not subject to the
whims of the market,

00:07:50.010 --> 00:07:52.260
but guaranteed rights to everyone,

00:07:52.260 --> 00:07:54.420
and a world, where in
the face of disaster,

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:56.760
rather than predatory
capitalist descending,

00:07:56.760 --> 00:07:58.980
the devastated communities themselves

00:07:58.980 --> 00:08:00.540
are empowered to rebuild.

00:08:00.540 --> 00:08:02.310
We see this happen to some extent

00:08:02.310 --> 00:08:04.170
when Randy stops being a Karen

00:08:04.170 --> 00:08:06.600
and decides to reembody who he used to be,

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:09.090
a talented scientist,
who's uniquely qualified

00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:10.770
to solve South Park's water problem.

00:08:10.770 --> 00:08:13.800
And after some public
cajoling and a massive deck,

00:08:13.800 --> 00:08:15.660
he gets the local government to pour money

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:17.940
into his ocean water desalination project.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:20.220
After a few less relevant plot points,

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:21.900
Randy eventually finds the solution

00:08:21.900 --> 00:08:23.190
to the town's water problem

00:08:23.190 --> 00:08:25.950
in Cartman's distractingly huge boobs.

00:08:25.950 --> 00:08:30.240
- How to get salt water to
the desalinization plant?

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:35.240
Salt water, sailing, saline
in the plant, saline implant!

00:08:36.630 --> 00:08:37.560
Holy (beep)!

00:08:37.560 --> 00:08:38.460
- [Michael] And at first glance,

00:08:38.460 --> 00:08:40.110
it seems like they simply found

00:08:40.110 --> 00:08:42.870
a Barstool Sports friendly
answer to their problem.

00:08:42.870 --> 00:08:44.610
It's sort of like ads
from power companies,

00:08:44.610 --> 00:08:47.580
